    ],
    "designDecisionsAndRationale": [
      "Reservation is the commit point so attempt, reserved session, lease, decision/request, placement evidence, and task advancement commit or roll back together.",
      "One-use digest-only launch authorization and durable terminal reconciliation prevent duplicate or uncertain execution from silently relaunching.",
      "The dispatcher orchestrates existing workflow execution while RielaCore remains independent of RielaWork.",
      "Guard batches are durably persisted before a total stable director decision so replay, budgets, causality, and completion remain evidence-backed.",
      "Reachable-node requirements and one fresh merged capability snapshot drive validation and placement; explicit assignment and pins never silently fall back.",
      "Task run/decide and equivalent dispatcher regressions ship in the same delivery cut as auto-improve removal to avoid a capability gap.",
      "Shared indexes, documentation, lockfiles, broad lint, example reconciliation, and package-digest work are deferred to serial finalization to prevent parallel overwrite."
    ],
    "intentionalTradeoffs": [
      "Explicitly enabled but unprobed backends may be usable as unverified, while failed authentication remains visible; strict host validation can reject these states.",
      "P1 uses existing worker/step topology and one bounded agent-director child rather than introducing a generic scheduler, proposal system, replanning layer, or specialist classifier.",
      "Historical retained implementation is reconciled and freshly verified rather than discarded or treated as accepted evidence."
    ],
    "supportedEdgeCases": [
      "Independent-connection reservation races, rollback at every transaction boundary, stale versions, missing versus unmet dependencies, duplicate identities, token replay, and terminal-unreconciled fencing.",
      "Decision replay conflicts, transaction-scoped pending requests, cancellation acknowledgment loss, stale launcher writes, guard equality boundaries, last-permitted-attempt completion, and full causal completion reconstruction.",
      "Reachability cycles, called workflows, unreachable nodes, pins and ordered fallback policies, model-list uncertainty, declaration/probe merges, stale snapshots, explicit worker assignments, and capacity waits.",
      "Read-only dry runs against absent, incompatible, or corrupt stores and profile configuration, including byte/row/sidecar equivalence.",
      "Interrupted task execution across authorization, running, cancellation acknowledgment, and pending rerun consumption."
